        "summary":  "Boston University research found that marketing AI agents as digital employees leads humans to catch 18% fewer errors and reduces accountability among workers. The study argues that framing AI tools as coworkers creates unrealistic expectations and encourages workers to abdicate responsibilityΓÇöthe opposite of what tech companies promoting this framing intend. The findings challenge the dominant industry narrative around AI as a collaborative teammate.",
        "execSummary":  "The coworker framing for AI agentsΓÇöpopularized by Salesforce, Microsoft, and othersΓÇöcarries a measurable organizational risk: it reduces human error-detection by 18% and diffuses accountability. For customer-support leaders deploying AI agents alongside human agents, this is a workforce design problem, not just a communications one. Job titles, process ownership, and escalation protocols must be structured to keep humans in a genuinely supervisory posture rather than a passive co-pilot role. Vendors who anthropomorphize their AI products are optimizing for adoption, not for the outcomes that matter to enterprise leaders.",
